    "q-293620": {
      "dom_id": "step-q293620",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}-(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6-4\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6-12} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{(-6)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(2\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6})}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
